{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfeJDqikssLfyeZomLYs8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I set up the project in `/work` and it works: all 6 tests pass.\n\n**Files:**\n- `package.json`: the project is named `range-to-regex`, with `npm test` and `npm start` scripts, a `bin` entry so it can run as a command, and Node 18 or newer required.\n- `range-to-regex.js`: you can import it as a module (`rangeToRegex('1-100')` returns a `RegExp`) or run it from the command line.\n- `range-to-regex.test.js`: the tests run every number around each range and check it matches if and only if it's inside the range. That covers `1-100`, `37-4521` and a negative range. They also check reversed bounds, extra characters like `0100`, `1000` and `50a`, and bad input.\n- `.gitignore`: ignores `node_modules/`.\n\n**Dependency:** I used [`to-regex-range`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/to-regex-range), the widely used package behind `micromatch`, rather than writing the digit-splitting logic myself. That logic is easy to get wrong at boundaries like 99→100. My code reads the `\"min-max\"` string, swaps the bounds if they're reversed, and anchors the pattern with `^…$` so `1-100` doesn't match part of `1000`.\n\n**Usage:**\n```\n$ node range-to-regex.js 1-100\n^(?:(?:[1-9]|[1-9]\\d|100))$\n\n$ node range-to-regex.js 37-4521\n^(?:3[7-9]|[4-9]\\d|[1-9]\\d{2}|[1-3]\\d{3}|4[0-4]\\d{2}|45[01]\\d|452[01])$\n```\n\nThe output wraps the pattern in a redundant extra group, `(?:(?:…))`. It doesn't change what matches; it's there so single values like `5-5` are still grouped correctly.\n\nThe pattern only matches whole integers: it rejects leading zeros (`007`), decimals and surrounding whitespace. If you'll be searching inside larger text instead of checking a whole string, swap `^…$` for word boundaries or lookarounds. I haven't committed anything."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usa